{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\":\"submission_c34.json\",\"summary\":\"Second scenario check (action=check) for the already-accepted requirement unit-claim-install-snapshot-alignment, testing the operational consequence rather than the bookkeeping alone; preflight-validated with valid=True and no diagnostics.\\n\\nDesign: plan_c7.json plus harness_snap2_test.go seed a log through index 5, install a snapshot at index 3 through the production handler, report the retained tail, then deliver the AppendEntries a leader sends next — anchored at the snapshot index (its nextIndex is snapshot index + 1 after a successful install) with entries conflicting with the tail — and report the acceptance decision, the resulting last entry, term and commit index. Declarative prerequisite: tail_retained true from the install event. Property: the anchored AppendEntries is accepted, identity op_id shared by both events, evaluated from emitted fields rather than tests_passed.\\n\\nConstruction evidence (not the formal observation): a local run in a copy of the captured module shows the tail after install (node_last_index 5, term 1, tail_retained true) and then append_success false with the last entry unchanged — the receiver rejects the leader's entries. The mechanism is sourced: because the effective last entry comes from the retained tail, the previous-log check falls through to the log store for the anchor index, which the install-time compaction removed.\\n\\nDeclared limits: real-leader reachability of the retained-tail state is still assumed rather than established; the leader's subsequent behaviour (nextIndex decrement, possibly another snapshot) was not measured; and the adaptations of the first scenario apply (direct main-thread handlers with skipStartup, a substitute answering restore futures, a seeded caller-owned log, TrailingLogs 0). No target code was modified.\\n\\nRun state: four confirmed in-scope violations; this product adds a second, sharper scenario for the snapshot finding whose outcome, if the fresh execution reproduces it, shows the misalignment can block replication rather than only misreport the last entry.\"}"}}
